## Project board (human+agent kanban)

**The coordination board is an Epic** in the **<BOARD_TEAM>** team of the **<BOARD_PROJECT>** project, scoped to area path **`<BOARD_AREA>`**, on Azure DevOps Server (on-prem). There is no GitHub Project and no `gh` here — this repo's only remote is ADO. Cards are **work items**: a **Feature** is "Unshaped" (the fluid plan), a **User Story** is a shaped, PR-sized unit `/next` works. Cards carry **coordination state only** — title, state, tags, assignee, and a link to the PR / notes file / HUMAN-REVIEW-QUEUE entry; knowledge stays in the md files. Card grain = attention-transfer events (human gates, PR-sized agent work) — never agent-internal micro-tasks. Tags: `agent-work` (Responsible = AI agent), `human-review` (a human decision/sign-off — see the status table), `stakeholder` (decision belongs to a non-account stakeholder; delivered by relay — the assignee shows them the packet and records the decision as comment-with-attribution + close). Rationale: `PROCESS.md`.

- **Tool-neutral procedures live in `process/`** (`next.md`, `kanban-check.md` + its implementation `kanban_check.sh` [read-only orientation report — the script is what `next` actually runs for its orientation passes; the md is its spec and by-hand fallback, carries no delegation policy of its own, and must stay self-contained: never make it cross-reference `next.md`, restate the rule instead], `board-setup.md`); the ADO REST transport is **`process/ado_api.sh`** (source it; it holds PAT auth, the api-version pin, and the `ADO_READONLY` guard). Per-tool entry points are thin wrappers: `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` (Claude Code), `.opencode/command/<name>.md` (OpenCode), `.codex/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` (Codex). Fallback for any tool without discovery: when the user says "next" (and only then), follow `process/next.md` directly.
- **The board is one Epic's subtree, and `/next` takes a scope.** <BOARD_PROJECT> holds ~22,779 work items, so a whole-project read is impossible. `/next <Epic | Feature | User Story id>` reconciles exactly one Epic's `Hierarchy-Forward` subtree (tens of items); given an Epic or Feature it picks the **lowest-numbered unclaimed User Story** in the subtree (D3), given a User Story it does that one. WIQL must use `MODE(Recursive)` — `MODE(MustContain)` returns only direct children and silently drops grandchildren.
- **Status is derived from `System.State` + tags** — the board columns are a view; the script never keys on board position:
  | Coordination status | Native state | Carrier |
  |---|---|---|
  | Unshaped | `Feature: New` | the Feature type |
  | Todo | `User Story: New` | User Story |
  | Agent working | `User Story: Active` | User Story |
  | Awaiting code review | `User Story: Resolved` | User Story, **no** `human-review` tag |
  | Awaiting human action | `User Story: Resolved` | **tag `human-review`** |
  | Done | `User Story: Closed` | User Story |

  Keep status truthful as you work: `Active` when you start a card (no PR yet), `Resolved` (no tag) when its draft PR opens, `Closed` when the human merges. A card left in Agent working by a session that stopped is invisible to every future session — `next`'s sync pass sweeps those back to `New` after 24 h. **"Awaiting code review" and "Awaiting human action" are both `Resolved` (a native split column on the Stories board); only the `human-review` tag tells them apart, so never move a card to Awaiting human action without the tag.**
- **Unshaped = a Feature** (a native work-item type, not a board-only stub): the fluid project plan, everything we believe we'll do, as a cheap one-liner (source + gate). **Shaping = writing a self-contained User Story under the Feature** with a body, tag (`agent-work` or `human-review`), assignee, then state `New`. **Shaping is human-triggered: agents draft, humans ratify** — an agent shapes only when a human asks (or offers and gets a yes); never write User Stories for a Feature unprompted. There is deliberately **no Blocked state**: blocked-on-a-human is Awaiting human action; blocked-on-other-work stays a Feature with its gate named.
- **Card bodies are self-contained:** the human action, and the agent work it unblocks, must be executable without the originating conversation — exact ask, where to look, what proceeds when cleared. If a card needs the full prior chat to make sense, it isn't ready to be a card.
- **Lead with the ask — every time.** A card transfers one decision to a person who has not read the conversation that produced it, so order matters more than length. **The first line of a new card's body is the ask itself** — the question or action, in plain language, understandable without opening a file; context, reasoning and evidence come after. **The last comment on a card must open the same way**, including "nothing right now, this is FYI" when that is the truth; if a comment changes what is being asked, the new ask goes on top. Plain language means no repo context: "Confirm `edu.example.app` is the final bundle identifier" is an ask; "resolve the HRQ #13 remainder" is not — spell out acronyms and card numbers on first use. A thread whose current ask can only be reconstructed by reading every comment in order has already failed. (This is also what lets `kanban_check.sh` report each card's ask mechanically.)
- **Anything a human must read to clear a gate is committed to the repo** — review packets, transcripts, rendered artifacts — tracked in git and linked from the card by permalink. "Regenerate it with this script" is not delivery: it strands the reviewer on whichever machine produced the artifact and makes the approved thing unrecoverable later. Give a gitignore rule that would swallow a reviewable artifact an explicit un-ignore. Sensitive or personal data stays out of git regardless — build packets from synthetic or already-public content.
- **Consolidated gate cards:** where one human decision would clear several gates at once, they collapse to one card with checkboxes rather than one card each, and a note at the top of `HUMAN-REVIEW-QUEUE.md` names the current consolidations. A new review question on a consolidated topic is a checkbox plus a comment on that card, not a new card. **This consolidates gates, not work** — distinct build work always gets its own card, including work arising from a consolidated topic. Test: a human *decides* it → consolidate; an agent *does* it → card it.
- **Every card gets an assignee (an ADO display name = the accountable human); assigning the wrong human beats assigning no one.** The **Responsible** distinction rides on the tags — `agent-work` = AI agent (the coding agent, any tool), `human-review` = a human decision/sign-off. **This is the deliberate carrier (decided 2026-10-16), not a stopgap:** a custom picklist field would be a process customization needing a Collection Admin, and the tags carry the same information with none of that.
- **Multi-developer rule: an agent *starts work* only on cards assigned to its own operator** (the operator is the PAT's user). Board bookkeeping and cleared-gate processing are **global and claim-guarded** (lane-scoping those would make follow-on work wait for a specific human to open a session); claiming = a nonce-stamped claim comment on the work item, **arbitrated by comment `id`** (claim → re-fetch → lowest id wins; ADO work-item fields have no atomic test-and-set), with state/assignee as the visible signal (Active when starting; reassign after winning the claim when poaching; `Processed:` comments on gates). Details: `process/next.md`.
- **Signals vs payload:** a card move is the signal; the substance (decision text, approval wording, feedback) goes in a work-item comment. A closed work item tagged `human-review` with no contrary comment = approved. A human comment on a still-OPEN gate is payload too: explicit complete decision → process it and close with attribution; **change request → re-queue, don't auto-start** (supersede the gate's claim marker, post an `Agent:` acknowledgment, card to `New` with tag `agent-work`; normal selection decides when it is worked — then `Active`, reply what changed, back to `Resolved` + `human-review`; never close for a human who isn't done deciding); question → answer; ambiguous → surface, never guess. Close is reserved for "my decision is complete."
- **Session pickup:** the `next` procedure (`process/next.md`, entry points above) — after claiming a card it **requires two human gates in every harness: an approved plan before any edit (step 5), and a fresh-eyes review + testing report / proposed manual test before the draft PR is opened (step 7)** — its orientation passes (steps 0–3) are produced by running `process/kanban_check.sh --scope <id>` directly in-session (read-only; the transport is pinned `ADO_READONLY=1` and refuses every mutation before it builds a request — it replaced delegating those passes to a local model, after delegates were measured inventing work-item numbers and writing to the checkout); judgment and all writes stay with the session — **run it only when the human invokes it** (the command, or "what's next?"); never orient against the board unprompted. **The deliverable is the work, not the orientation report:** a turn ends on a **draft** PR or a recorded blocker — never on a claim comment and a board move alone, and never with the agent promoting a draft or merging (those are the human's acts). Read a card's comments at pickup — guidance lands there. Don't double-card: a PR already tracked by a work item in Awaiting code review gets no second card. Mid-session, watch a card you're blocked on with a background polling loop (Claude Code: the Monitor tool; other tools: a shell loop, or re-run next when you return). When no session is running, moves simply queue — the board is a mailbox, not a pager.
- **Plumbing — the ADO REST recipe** (PAT with `vso.work_write`, `vso.code_write`, `vso.project`; the transport is `process/ado_api.sh`; api-version `6.0`, comments `6.0-preview`):
  - **Create a card**: `POST {base}/{project}/_apis/wit/workitems/$<Type>?api-version=6.0` (types `$Epic`, `$Feature`, `$User Story`) with a JSON-patch body adding `System.Title`, `System.AreaPath`, and (for a User Story) `System.Description` + a `System.Links.LinkType` parent link to its Feature. **The board is the Epic's subtree, so a card that is not parented into the Epic does not appear** — always link it.
  - **Move status**: `PATCH {base}/{project}/_apis/wit/workitems/<id>?api-version=6.0` with `[{"op":"add","path":"/fields/System.State","value":"Active"}]`.
  - **Tag**: patch `System.Tags`. **`add` appends** a tag; **`replace` sets the whole set** (read the current tags first and re-send all of them). There is **no standalone create-tag call on this server** (`POST/PATCH/PUT/DELETE …/_apis/wit/tags` → 405); a tag is created implicitly by the first work item that carries it.
  - **Assign**: patch `System.AssignedTo` to the operator's display name (ADO resolves the identity).
  - **Comment**: `POST {base}/{project}/_apis/wit/workItems/<id>/comments?api-version=6.0-preview` with `{"text":"…"}`.
  - **The only unbounded fetch is the PR list** (`$top=1000` in the transport); a full page is ambiguous, so `kanban_check.sh` prints a `LIMITS:` line rather than aborting. The subtree read is bounded (WIQL `MODE(Recursive)`) and the work-items batch is chunked at ADO's 200-id cap.

## PR flow

**The agent opens a *draft* PR and stops there. Only the human promotes it to ready-for-review, and only the human merges.** `POST {base}/{project}/_apis/git/repositories/<repo-id>/pullrequests?api-version=6.0` with `isDraft: true` on a feature branch (`issue-<n>[-slug]`), with the testing report (and any manual test) in the description, linked from the card; post an `Agent:` comment whose first line says what landed, where to look, and that it awaits the human's promotion. `Fixes #<n>` in the PR description moves the work item to `Resolved` on merge. The card moves to **Awaiting code review** (state `Resolved`, no `human-review` tag) the moment the draft PR is posted — a draft PR is the agent's end state, and the human's turn is to review it, promote it to ready-for-review (`PATCH …/pullrequests/<id>` `{"isDraft": false}`), and merge (`PATCH …` `{"status":"completed","lastMergeSourceCommit":…,"completionOptions":{"mergeStrategy":"squash"}}`). Never promote a draft, never merge, and never mark a draft ready on the human's behalf.

**Review of the promoted PR is the human's side of the process, not the agent's.** A human reviewer acts after promotion; the agent does not poll for that review, does not re-request it, and does not act on its findings unless the human asks. The process's own review is the context-blind fresh-eyes review *before* the draft PR (`next` step 7); never make any single reviewer a hard dependency.

**Branch naming: `issue-<n>[-slug]`.** `next`'s stale-claim sweep asks "is there a remote branch for this card?" and answers it by matching that prefix — a branch named anything else reads as no branch at all, and a card with real work behind it gets swept back to Todo.

**After the human merges, clean up locally — and do not trust `git branch --merged`.** ADO's `deleteSourceBranch` deletes only the **remote** branch. Delete the local branch and remove any worktree you made for the work in the same breath as merging. The usual "delete merged branches" idiom is actively misleading under squash merging: squashing rewrites the commits, so a merged branch is **never** an ancestor of `main` and `git branch --merged origin/main` lists only `main` itself — no feature branch ever appears, which reads as "all clean" while they pile up. Decide by PR state instead (`GET …/pullrequests?searchCriteria.status=completed`, or `git ls-remote --heads origin`) and delete with `git branch -D`. A worktree also **pins** its branch, so a branch sweep silently skips whatever a worktree holds — remove the worktree first (`git worktree remove <path> && git worktree prune`), then the branch, then `git remote prune origin`. **Review-scratch branches count** (a PR checked out locally to look at it): they sit on *intermediate* commits of a merged PR, so they are patch-contained but invisible to every ancestry check, and they are the easiest kind to forget. Before deleting, confirm nothing unique is there — no uncommitted changes, and every commit accounted for in some completed PR's commit list; `git cherry` cannot answer this, because squashing changes the patch id. Never delete a worktree you cannot inspect, and never report as gone anything you did not verify.

## Human review queue

`HUMAN-REVIEW-QUEUE.md` holds the full writeups and packets for human gates (review materials, what each gates, effort). **Every open item there has a board card** (tag `human-review`, assignee = the human, state `Resolved` = Awaiting human action when materials are ready) — *except* items covered by a consolidated tracking card, which get a comment on that card instead (see "Consolidated gate cards" above; a note at the top of this file names the current consolidations, and the match is by **category**, not by a list of enumerated items). Check items off as the humans complete them. Anything a reviewer must read to decide is committed to the repo and linked by permalink, never regenerated on demand.
